# Train a whole-body @@TITLE@@ motion-tracking policy

A Unitree G1 has to reproduce a 20-second excerpt of LAFAN1 `@@MOTION@@`: 29
joints, 50 Hz, matching the reference whole-body motion and world-frame
trajectory without falling over.

**The environment and a reference PPO are given. What you train is your choice.**
The complete one-hour baseline is `/workspace/reference_ppo.py`. It runs as-is
and continually stages its best-known export in `/logs/artifacts/policy/`:

```bash
python3 /workspace/reference_ppo.py
```

Treat it as readable starter code: run it, copy and modify it, or replace it
with another method. It is deliberately ordinary rather than an optimal
solution. Any method is legal: on-policy RL, behaviour cloning from the
reference motion, a hybrid objective, distillation, or anything else you can
build from torch and the environment you are given.

## What you ship

A policy at `/logs/artifacts/policy/`:

```
/logs/artifacts/policy/
├── policy.onnx        # required
└── policy_meta.json   # required
```

Write it with `harness.export.export(actor, path, ...)`, which
traces your network and records the metadata. If you maintain observation
normalisation outside the actor, pass it as `obs_mean` and `obs_std`; the exporter
bakes those statistics into the graph. `export.validate(path)` tells you whether
the result is acceptable before you rely on it.

**Stage early, stage often.** Whatever is in `/logs/artifacts/policy/` when your
session ends is what gets scored. Re-export after every improvement; a better
policy still sitting in your workspace scores nothing.

## Budgets

- **Four hours total wall clock**, on one GPU. Training, evaluation and restarts
  all consume the same session. The reference recipe runs roughly 4,000
  iterations per hour at 4,096 environments, about 400M environment steps.
  Treat the four hours as an optimization budget: keep your best checkpoint
  staged and keep improving it until the session ends.
- **10,000,000 parameters** in the exported graph, counted from the file. Over
  budget is rejected outright, not scaled down.

## The contract you cannot change

The evaluator runs your policy in a simulator it builds itself, so the interface
between you and it is fixed. All of it is in `/workspace/harness/spec.py`:

- **Observation**, 160 floats, in this order: the reference joint positions and
  velocities for the current frame (58), the reference anchor position (3) and
  orientation (6) in the robot's torso frame, root linear (3) and angular (3)
  velocity in the root frame, measured joint positions relative to the default
  pose (29), joint velocities (29), and your previous action (29).
- **Action**, 29 floats: joint position targets, as an offset from the default
  pose scaled per joint by `robot.ACTION_SCALE`.
- **50 Hz control.**
- Your policy may consume a stack of the last N observations (1 to 16). Pass N
  as `obs_history=N` when you export; the exporter records it as
  `obs_history_length` in `policy_meta.json`. That is the only thing about your
  own interface you get to declare.

Everything else is yours: network architecture, losses, optimiser, reward terms
and their weights, terminations, domain randomization, how you sample start
states, whether you use the rest of the dataset.

## What you are given

- `harness.env.TrackingEnv` — thousands of G1s stepping in
  parallel on the GPU (MuJoCo-Warp). Reward terms, terminations and adaptive
  start-state sampling are in `mdp.py` as plain functions over batched tensors;
  override, reweight or ignore them. `env.privileged_observation()` gives a
  critic a fuller view than the policy is allowed.
- `harness.evaluator` — the evaluation loop itself, so you can
  measure yourself the way you will be measured.
- `harness.progress` — a training log and an `evaluate()` helper
  that scores a staged policy on the design seeds.
- `$MOTIONTRACK_MOTION` — the scored clip, already converted.
- `/workspace/assets/lafan1/` — all 40 G1-retargeted LAFAN1 clips. Convert any
  of them with `motion.convert(...)`.
- `/workspace/assets/unitree_g1/` — the robot.

```bash
python3 dev_eval.py /logs/artifacts/policy          # score yourself
python3 dev_eval.py /logs/artifacts/policy --video /logs/run.mp4
```

Design seeds `101, 211, 307` are yours to develop against.

## How you are scored

Your checkpoint is re-run in a **different simulator**: MuJoCo-C at a 2 ms
timestep, where training used MuJoCo-Warp at 5 ms. Hidden seeds perturb the
robot, sensors and ground, and apply pushes. Only the re-executed policy is
scored.

Episodes reward multi-scale whole-body position tracking and survival; jerky
actions and falls are penalised. Tracking uses 0.3, 0.1 and 0.05 m kernels so
centimetre-level differences remain visible. Tracking credit is averaged over
the full clip, with post-fall frames scoring zero. World-frame anchor drift is
reported only as a diagnostic and is not scored. Scores are averaged over the
evaluation episodes.

An episode ends if the torso height error exceeds 0.25 m, its tilt error exceeds
0.8, or a hand or foot height error exceeds 0.25 m. The submission must load,
match the interface, fit the parameter budget, remain finite, and be
deterministic: repeating an episode must reproduce it exactly.

There is no network access. Everything you need is in this container.
